#====================================================================
# DRAFT - Desktop framework-bound dependency closure on OHOS: contract-anchored reimplementation with a native C API backend
#====================================================================

id:                     DRAFT-macos-framework-closure-ohos-capi-backend
trigger_tool:           clang|conan|cmake|scout
phase:                  porting|building
error_signature:        Foundation/Foundation.h.*not found|fatal error: .*objc/objc.h|fatal error: .*Cocoa/Cocoa.h|framework not found|cannot find -lobjc|undefined symbol: _objc_

severity:               2
category:               platform, dependency
match_keywords:         macOS, Objective-C, Cocoa, Carbon, CoreGraphics, CGEvent, dependency closure, libobjc, objc headers, GNUstep, sysroot probe, pure C reimplementation, OHOS C API, native backend, CLI contract, upstream test anchoring
title:                  When sources are bound to desktop-only frameworks and the OHOS sysroot has no ObjC runtime the dependency closure is unsatisfiable: reimplement the CLI contract in portable C anchored by upstream tests with an OHOS native C API backend, and keep GNUstep only as a fallback closure

root_cause:             The OHOS native sysroot ships no ObjC runtime: usr/include/objc does not exist and no libobjc is present under usr/lib/aarch64-linux-ohos, so #import <Foundation/Foundation.h> and -framework Cocoa/Carbon cannot compile or link. The closure gap cannot be bypassed with build flags; it forces an explicit route decision. Family practice distinguishes three cases: cross-platform projects grow an OHOS native backend on their existing platform abstraction; partially portable CLIs keep the portable core and add a backend with capability split; fully framework-bound sources must stay in-tree uncompiled for provenance while the observable CLI contract is reimplemented in portable C over OHOS C APIs.

affected_scope:         Any project whose upstream sources bind to desktop-only frameworks (Objective-C/Cocoa/Carbon/CoreGraphics/CGEvent and similar) and that must be delivered on HarmonyOS PC where the sysroot lacks the matching runtime.

verification_cmd:       In a fresh device shell: (1) test ! -e /data/service/hnp/ohos-sdk.org/ohos-sdk_26.0.0.18/ohos/native/sysroot/usr/include/objc must pass (no ObjC headers); (2) ls /data/service/hnp/ohos-sdk.org/ohos-sdk_26.0.0.18/ohos/native/sysroot/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-ohos | grep -i objc must print nothing (no libobjc); (3) printf '#import <Foundation/Foundation.h>\nint main(void){return 0;}\n' > probe_objc.m && /data/service/hnp/bin/clang --target=aarch64-linux-ohos --sysroot=/data/service/hnp/ohos-sdk.org/ohos-sdk_26.0.0.18/ohos/native/sysroot -fsyntax-only probe_objc.m must fail with 'Foundation/Foundation.h' file not found.

rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           contract-anchored-reimplementation-with-native-capi-backend
rec_description:        Keep the family-level decision table; do not narrow the pattern to a single face. (1) Cross-platform projects add an OHOS native backend on their existing abstraction: synergy-core 1.20.4 consumes oh_input_manager.h in its OHOS platform layer (patches 0004/0027/0028/0042; 0027 adds src/lib/platform/OHOSKeyState.cpp) — cite it as a native-backend injection-consumer practice, NOT as a closure-unsatisfiable case, since its OHOS backend was not forced by a macOS closure. (2) Partially portable CLIs keep the portable core, add the OHOS backend and split capabilities with negative tests: screenresolution 1.6 (E240, CoreGraphics read-only queries onto OH_NativeDisplayManager). (3) Fully framework-bound sources (100% ObjC) cannot link any route: keep macOS sources in-tree uncompiled for provenance, pin the observable CLI contract by porting the upstream test suite 1:1 as oracle, reimplement that contract in portable C over OHOS C APIs as an additive backend, and give every capability boundary an explicit unsupported diagnostic with a negative test.
rec_command:            Probe the sysroot first (verification_cmd), then pick the tier. Anchor the reimplementation on upstream tests: portable assertions 1:1, platform-blocked assertions replaced only with a named reason, boundaries asserted as explicit unsupported dual-outcome tests.
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               sysroot-probe-then-contract-tests-plus-pure-c-backend

fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      gnustep-substitute-closure
fallback_description:   If ObjC sources must actually execute (not just their CLI contract), a GNUstep stack (libobjc2 + gnustep-base/gnustep-gui) can substitute parts of the ObjC closure. Honest tradeoffs: heavyweight new dependency closure on OHOS, headless/service behavior uncertain, and the CGEvent/Carbon injection layer still needs a full re-host, so most of the porting work remains. Fallback only; never the default route for small CLIs.
fallback_command:       raise ConanInvalidConfiguration("GNUstep toolchain not found on host; GNUstep route is fallback-only, choose contract-anchored reimplementation")
fallback_auto_fix:      false
fallback_impl:          recipe-validate-guard

forbid_1_pattern:       git rm.*\.m|delete.*macos.*sources|rm -rf.*src/.*\.m
forbid_1_reason:        Deleting upstream desktop sources from the delivered tree breaks provenance and makes the port unauditable against upstream.
forbid_1_consequence:   Reviewers cannot diff the port against upstream; the delivery violates source-completeness rules.

forbid_2_pattern:       stub.*return 0|ifdef __OHOS__.*pass|no.*negative.*test.*boundary
forbid_2_reason:        Silent stubs that return success without negative tests fake the platform capability boundary.
forbid_2_consequence:   CI and users believe unsupported commands work; failures surface in production instead of test_package.

forbid_3_pattern:       default.*GNUstep|use_gnustep.*True.*default
forbid_3_reason:        GNUstep substitutes only part of the closure and leaves CGEvent/Carbon layers unhosted, so as a default route it multiplies work for small CLIs.
forbid_3_consequence:   Long GNUstep bring-up for a CLI whose observable contract could be reimplemented in portable C in days.

principles:             P-TOOLCHAIN-001, P-SYSCALL-001, P-RUNTIME-001

